From c87ad54b20ebe8b5f7bc520fbd39f0b9113c4d90 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LuneOS <webos-ports@googlegroups.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 19:40:10 +0200
Subject: Support metadata spanning multiple block devices

Devices that launched before Android 10 and were later upgraded use *retrofit*
dynamic partitions: rather than one dedicated "super", the logical partitions
are spread across the pre-existing physical ones. The Pixel 3a is a case in
point - its board config sets

    BOARD_SUPER_PARTITION_METADATA_DEVICE := system
    BOARD_SUPER_PARTITION_BLOCK_DEVICES   := system vendor

so there is no partition named "super" at all, and extents reference two
different block devices.

The tool refused this outright:

    if (extent.target_source != 0) {
      cerr << "This utility does not yet support multiple block devices";
      return 1;
    }

and hardcoded argv[1] as the device in every linear target, so even the first
device was only correct by coincidence.

Resolve each extent's target_source through metadata->block_devices instead.
Index 0 keeps using the path the metadata was read from, so the common
single-super case is byte-for-byte unchanged. Other indices are looked up by
their metadata name beside that path, inheriting its A/B slot suffix
(/dev/disk/by-partlabel/system_a therefore finds .../vendor_a) - but only when
the recorded name does not already carry that suffix, which on a real device it
does. An optional name=path argument overrides the lookup entirely, for porting
and testing.

Upstream-Status: Pending
